{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act statutory grounds for eviction","requirement":"A landlord may evict a residential tenant only on the statutory grounds listed in the Anti-Eviction Act and must serve the notice the Act sets for each ground before filing suit; a court judgment is required.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings, complexes, and rooming and boarding homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner-occupied two- or three-unit premises with two or fewer rental units may be exempt"},"exemptions":"May not apply to owner-occupied two- or three-unit premises with two or fewer rental units; transient or seasonal hotel, motel or guest-house guests; and a unit held in trust for a permanently occupying developmentally disabled immediate family member.","penalty":"An eviction for illegal occupancy requires the landlord to pay relocation assistance of six times the tenant's monthly rent; unpaid amounts accrue 18% annual interest and become a lien on the property.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The Anti-Eviction Act applies to most residential rental properties including single-family homes, mobile homes and land in a mobile home park, apartment buildings, and complexes. This law also applies to rooming and boarding homes (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-53 to -84).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord may not padlock, disconnect utilities, block entry or otherwise remove a tenant without the tenant's consent or a court judgment; only a court officer may physically evict a tenant after a warrant for removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenants in New Jersey, against any landlord or person acting on the landlord's behalf.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord or other person who enters and removes a tenant without a court order may be liable for damages to the tenant, and may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"Self-help evictions, or lockouts, made by the landlord are illegal in New Jersey.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
